<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AnalysisMode>AllEnabledByDefault</AnalysisMode>
    <CodeAnalysisRuleSet>../jellyfin.ruleset</CodeAnalysisRuleSet>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The server package set, and the line it belongs to is recorded here rather than
    left to be read off the number.

    10.11.0 is the first release of the 10.11 line, which is the current stable server
    line and the one this artifact declares in build.yaml. The line is the first two
    positions; the third is the build, and the pin is the line's FIRST build rather
    than its newest.

    That direction is the opposite of the obvious one and it is the whole reason this
    paragraph exists. A package compiled against a newer build binds the server
    libraries at that build, and a server between the declared floor and it admits the
    plugin on the floor and then refuses every type in it. 0.1.0.0 shipped exactly that
    pair, declaring 10.11.0.0 and binding 10.11.11.0, and CHANGELOG.md carries what an
    operator saw. So this version and the targetAbi in build.yaml are one value written
    twice, and moving either one alone is the defect rather than an inconsistency.

    APackageSetPinnedAboveTheDeclaredAbiIsRefused is the guard, with the released pair
    as its near miss, and APackageSetPinnedAtTheDeclaredAbiIsAccepted is its neighbour.

    The package says which framework that line runs on rather than this file guessing
    it:

        curl -s https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/jellyfin.controller/10.11.0/jellyfin.controller.nuspec
          targetFramework="net9.0"

    which is the TargetFramework above.

    The second supported line, 12.0, is not referenced here. Its packages are release
    candidates and nothing else, and whether this repository pins one is answered on
    #1 rather than here: answer 5, taken 2026-08-24, is that 1.0 ships the 10.11
    artifact only and the 12.0 artifact follows a stable 12.0 server release, so no
    release candidate is pinned. #4 is where the second reference and the second
    framework arrive, and it stays unstarted on that half until that release exists.

    ExcludeAssets runtime on both, because a plugin is loaded beside a server that
    already carries these assemblies. The test project references the same two with
    their runtime assets kept, at the same versions, and the pair is held together by
    a suite reading both project files rather than by memory.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Controller" Version="10.11.0" >
      <ExcludeAssets>runtime</ExcludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Model" Version="10.11.0">
      <ExcludeAssets>runtime</ExcludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The store splits its write into a staged half and a commit half so a test can
    stop between them. Both are internal, because a caller that can stage without
    committing can leave a pending file behind, and the suite is the only thing that
    should be able to do that.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="SerilogAnalyzer" Version="0.15.0" PrivateAssets="All" />
    <PackageReference Include="StyleCop.Analyzers" Version="1.2.0-beta.556" PrivateAssets="All" />
    <PackageReference Include="SmartAnalyzers.MultithreadingAnalyzer" Version="1.1.31" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Remove="Configuration\configPage.html" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Configuration\configPage.html" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
